<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="galactolipid biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-401" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;B&gt;General Background&lt;/B&gt; The |FRAME: Galactolipids galactolipids| |FRAME: D-Galactosyl-12-diacyl-glycerols monogalactosyldiacylglycerol| (MGDG) and |FRAME: Galactosyl-galactosyl-diacyl-glycerols digalactosyldiacylglycerol| (DGDG) are prevalent membrane lipids in organisms that carry out oxygenic photosynthesis, such as land plants, algae, and cyanobacteria |CITS: [Dormann01]|. MGDG and DGDG constitute about 50% and 30%, respectively, of the total lipids in the majority of photosynthetic membranes. Crystallographic analysis of photosystem complexes revealed that stoichiometric amounts of both types of galactolipids are bound in the interior of photosystem complexes |CITS: [16963124][21499260]|, and it has been shown that a galactolipid-deficient mutant of |FRAME: TAX-3702| lacks photosynthetic activity |CITS: [17940034]|. &lt;B&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/B&gt; The biosynthesis of galactolipids in plants is localized to the envelope membranes of chloroplasts. Thus both MGDG and DGDG have to be transported to the thylakoid membranes during chloroplast development. The glycosylglycerides are synthesized using |FRAME: DIACYLGLYCEROL diacylglycerol| (DAG) as the starting compound. The first enzyme of galactolipid synthesis, |FRAME: EC-2.4.1.46|, was isolated from cucumber seedlings |CITS: [8990209]|. This enzyme transfers a galactose residue from |FRAME: CPD-14553| onto diacylglycerol. The enzyme inverts the anomeric configuration of the galactose, resulting in a &amp;beta; bond to DAG. Three genes encoding MGDG synthases were isolated from Arabidopsis |CITS: [11553816]|. However, only |FRAME: AT4G31780| is crucial for the synthesis of most of the MGDG lipid required for thylakoid membrane assembly in chloroplasts. |FRAME: AT5G20410| and |FRAME: AT2G11810| are only expressed in certain plant organs or under specific conditions such as phosphate limitation |CITS: [18808455]|. The second enzyme involved is |FRAME: EC-2.4.1.241|. Arabidopsis contains two genes encoding the enzyme - |FRAME: AT3G11670| and |FRAME: AT4G00550| |CITS: [10381884]|. Both are UDP-galactose-dependent galactosyltransferases that transfer galactose from |FRAME: CPD-14553| onto MGDG with retention of the anomeric configuration of the sugar moiety. In addition to MGDG and DGDG, plants contain low amounts of oligogalactolipids with three or four galactose residues in their head groups. The synthesis of these oligogalactolipids is independent of DGDG synthases |FRAME: AT3G11670| and |FRAME: AT4G00550|, and is carried out by |FRAME: EC-2.4.1.184| (GGGT) |CITS: [12743031]|, which is encoded by the |FRAME: AT3G06510| gene |CITS: [20798281]|. GGGT is not related to glycosyltransferases and belongs to the family of glycosylhydrolases. The enzyme acts on MGDG, converting it into di-, tri-, and tetragalactosyldiacylglycerol during low temperature stress. The galactosyl linkages are all in &amp;beta; form |CITS: [12743031]|. The GGGT activity provides an important mechanism for the stabilization of the chloroplast membranes during freezing and drought stress. SFR2 is essential for the adaptation of Arabidopsis to freezing stress |CITS: [15258268][18466306]|</Description>
